  "id": "upper_rio_negro_tukanoan_ritual",
  "name": "Upper Rio Negro ritual music (Tukano, Desana, Baniwa)",
  "family": "global",
  "lineage": "Ritual music of the Tukanoan peoples, among them the Tukano and Desana, and their Arawak-speaking Baniwa neighbours on the upper Rio Negro and Vaupés on the Brazil–Colombia border. Sacred Jurupari flutes, played in pairs by initiated men and kept from women's sight, sound in initiation rites, while the dabucuri exchange feasts between groups bring dances with cariçu panpipes, sung chants and rattles. Acácio Tadeu de Camargo Piedade studied Tukano (Ye'pâ-masa) music, and Brazilian and Colombian field recordings document the repertoire. A generic flute card stands in for both the sacred flutes and the cariçu panpipes.",
  "recipe": "classic modern upper rio negro ritual music, ritual Devotional, amazonian branch. flute like credited flute unspecified, maracas, foot stomp. field recording. ORTF stereo pair, portable field-recorder pre. 24-bit digital.",
